---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import '../../styles/marketing.css';
import {getComparison} from '../../data/comparisons';

const c = getComparison('github-copilot')!;

const title = 'Self-Hosted GitHub Copilot Agent Alternative | GratefulAgents';
const description =
  'GratefulAgents vs GitHub Copilot coding agent: run AI agents in your own Kubernetes cluster, keep credentials in-house, and choose your own models.';

const faqs = c.faqs;

const structuredData = [
  {
    '@type': 'TechArticle',
    '@id': 'https://gratefulagents.dev/alternatives/github-copilot/#article',
    headline: title,
    description,
    url: 'https://gratefulagents.dev/alternatives/github-copilot/',
    author: {'@type': 'Organization', '@id': 'https://gratefulagents.dev/#organization'},
    publisher: {'@id': 'https://gratefulagents.dev/#organization'},
  },
  {
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    '@id': 'https://gratefulagents.dev/alternatives/github-copilot/#breadcrumb',
    itemListElement: [
      {
        '@type': 'ListItem',
        position: 1,
        name: 'Home',
        item: 'https://gratefulagents.dev/',
      },
      {
        '@type': 'ListItem',
        position: 2,
        name: 'Alternatives',
        item: 'https://gratefulagents.dev/alternatives/',
      },
      {
        '@type': 'ListItem',
        position: 3,
        name: 'GitHub Copilot',
        item: 'https://gratefulagents.dev/alternatives/github-copilot/',
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    '@id': 'https://gratefulagents.dev/alternatives/github-copilot/#faq',
    mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: f.question,
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: f.answer,
      },
    })),
  },
];
---

<Base {title} {description} type="article" {structuredData}>
  <main id="main">
    <div class="mk-page">

      <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <ol class="mk-breadcrumb">
          <li><a href="/">Home</a></li>
          <li><a href="/alternatives/">Alternatives</a></li>
          <li><span aria-current="page">GitHub Copilot</span></li>
        </ol>
      </nav>

      <div class="mk-layout-2col">
        <div>

          <div class="mk-hero">
            <div class="mk-vs-badge">
              <span class="mk-vs-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
              Comparison
            </div>
            <h1>GratefulAgents vs GitHub Copilot Coding Agent</h1>
            <p>
              GitHub Copilot's coding agent is a cloud-managed service that executes inside
              GitHub's infrastructure. GratefulAgents is a self-hosted, open-source alternative
              that runs the same class of autonomous coding tasks inside your own Kubernetes
              cluster. This page compares them honestly—including where Copilot is the better
              choice.
            </p>
          </div>

          <article class="mk-prose">

            <h2>What GitHub Copilot's coding agent is</h2>
            <p>
              GitHub Copilot is Microsoft's AI developer platform, embedded throughout the
              GitHub.com product surface: inline code suggestions in editors, pull request summaries,
              code review comments, and an autonomous coding agent that can take an issue and
              produce a working pull request with minimal human input.
            </p>
            <p>
              The coding agent feature lets you assign a GitHub issue to Copilot. It clones the
              repository, analyses the codebase, writes a plan, implements changes, and opens a
              pull request—iterating based on CI feedback and review comments. All of this runs on
              GitHub's managed infrastructure. You do not provision servers or configure anything
              beyond enabling the feature in your GitHub organisation settings.
            </p>
            <p>
              Copilot's coding agent is backed by GitHub's deep repository context: it has access
              to your entire commit history, issue tracker, PR discussion, and code intelligence
              graph. No other vendor has equivalent first-party access to that signal.
            </p>

            <h2>What GitHub Copilot's coding agent is genuinely good at</h2>
            <p>
              {c.whatItIsGoodAt}
            </p>
            <p>
              If you want zero infrastructure work and your code already lives in GitHub cloud,
              Copilot's coding agent is the lower-friction option. The integration between the
              agent, the issue tracker, and the PR review surface is seamless in a way that a
              third-party tool cannot fully replicate. For individuals, startups, and teams without
              data-residency requirements, it is a strong default choice.
            </p>

            <h2>What changes when you need self-hosted execution</h2>
            <p>
              The cloud-managed model has architectural implications that matter in certain
              contexts:
            </p>
            <ul>
              <li>
                <strong>Your source code leaves your network.</strong> Every time the coding agent
                runs, it clones your repository onto GitHub's infrastructure. For many open-source
                and startup codebases this is unremarkable—the code is already public or already
                lives in GitHub's systems. For regulated, financial-services, government, or
                strictly IP-protective organisations, it may not be permissible.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Model credentials are managed by Microsoft.</strong> You do not control
                which model is used, at what version, or what data is sent to the model provider.
                Your prompt content and code snippets transit Microsoft's and the underlying model
                provider's infrastructure.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Private dependencies are inaccessible.</strong> If your build requires
                packages from a private registry, services on an internal network, or resources
                behind a corporate firewall, the GitHub-hosted agent cannot reach them.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Observability is the product UI.</strong> You see what GitHub shows you.
                If you need a full distributed trace of every model call, tool invocation, and
                token expenditure for audit or cost-control purposes, the Copilot UI does not
                provide that depth.
              </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
              None of these limitations are bugs—they are the natural consequences of the
              cloud-managed architecture. For organisations where those constraints apply,
              self-hosting is the only viable path.
            </p>

            <h2>Dimension-by-dimension comparison</h2>
            <p>
              The table below compares the two tools across stable architectural dimensions,
              not surface features that may change between product releases.
            </p>

            <div class="mk-table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Comparison table: GratefulAgents vs GitHub Copilot coding agent">
              <table class="mk-compare-table">
                <thead>
                  <tr>
                    <th scope="col">Dimension</th>
                    <th scope="col">GitHub Copilot coding agent</th>
                    <th scope="col">GratefulAgents</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  {c.rows.map((row) => (
                    <tr>
                      <td>{row.dimension}</td>
                      <td>{row.competitor}</td>
                      <td>{row.gratefulagents}</td>
                    </tr>
                  ))}
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>

            <h2>Who should pick which</h2>

            <h3>Choose GitHub Copilot's coding agent if:</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>Your repositories are on GitHub.com and you have no data-residency constraints.</li>
              <li>You want a capable agent with zero infrastructure setup and immediate availability.</li>
              <li>You are an individual, a small team, or a startup where ops overhead is a real cost.</li>
              <li>Tight integration between the agent and GitHub's native code review surfaces matters to you.</li>
            </ul>

            <h3>Choose GratefulAgents if:</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>Your organisation has data-residency, IP-protection, or regulatory constraints that prevent source code from transiting third-party infrastructure.</li>
              <li>You need to reach private registries, internal APIs, or network-restricted services during agent runs.</li>
              <li>You want to bring your own model API keys and apply your own volume contracts for cost control.</li>
              <li>You need audit-grade observability: full distributed traces of every model call, tool invocation, and cost for every run.</li>
              <li>You want a Kubernetes-native, declarative approach to agent scheduling and trigger management.</li>
            </ul>

            <div class="mk-callout mk-callout-neutral">
              <strong>Note on current status:</strong> GratefulAgents is in early development.
              Bugs are expected, and there is no documented high-availability configuration,
              managed hosting, or production support SLA. Evaluate it with that in mind—run
              it in a non-production environment first and validate it against your workload.
            </div>

            <h2>How to try GratefulAgents</h2>
            <p>
              The fastest evaluation path is a local Kubernetes cluster using Kind. Docker Desktop
              (or Docker Engine) is the only prerequisite. The
              <a href="/docs/getting-started/self-hosting-kind/">Kind getting-started guide</a>
              walks through bootstrapping the cluster, deploying the Helm chart, adding model
              credentials, and triggering a first agent run against a GitHub repository—all in
              about fifteen minutes.
            </p>
            <p>
              For a persistent installation on a server, the
              <a href="/docs/getting-started/self-hosting-k3s/">k3s guide</a> covers deploying
              on a fresh Debian or Ubuntu machine.
            </p>
            <div class="cta-row">
              <a href="/docs/getting-started/self-hosting-kind/" class="btn btn-primary">
                Get started with Kind →
              </a>
              <a href="/alternatives/" class="btn btn-quiet">More comparisons</a>
            </div>

            <hr class="mk-divider" />

            <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
            <div class="mk-faq">
              {faqs.map((f) => (
                <details class="mk-faq-item">
                  <summary>{f.question}</summary>
                  <div class="mk-faq-answer">{f.answer}</div>
                </details>
              ))}
            </div>

          </article>

          <div class="mk-disclaimer">
            <p>
              GitHub® and GitHub Copilot™ are trademarks of GitHub, Inc. All trademarks are the
              property of their respective owners. This comparison reflects the authors' understanding
              of each product at time of writing; product capabilities change frequently. Consult
              <a href="https://github.com/features/copilot" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub's own Copilot documentation</a>
              for authoritative and up-to-date information.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>

        <aside>
          <div class="mk-sidebar-card">
            <h3>Try GratefulAgents</h3>
            <p>Self-hosted. Open source. Runs in your Kubernetes cluster.</p>
            <div class="cta-row">
              <a href="/docs/getting-started/self-hosting-kind/" class="btn btn-primary">
                Kind quick-start →
              </a>
              <a href="/docs/getting-started/self-hosting-k3s/" class="btn btn-quiet">
                k3s on a server
              </a>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="mk-sidebar-card" style="margin-top: 1rem;">
            <h3>More comparisons</h3>
            <p>See how GratefulAgents compares to other coding agent options.</p>
            <div class="cta-row">
              <a href="/alternatives/devin/" class="btn btn-quiet">vs Devin →</a>
              <a href="/alternatives/openhands/" class="btn btn-quiet">vs OpenHands →</a>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="mk-sidebar-card" style="margin-top: 1rem;">
            <h3>Why self-host?</h3>
            <p>Architecture guide covering data residency, IP protection, and cost control.</p>
            <div class="cta-row">
              <a href="/self-hosted-coding-agents/" class="btn btn-quiet">Read guide →</a>
            </div>
          </div>
        </aside>
      </div>

    </div>
  </main>
</Base>
